            </thead>
            <tbody class="text-gray-700">
              <tr class="bg-[#F8F8F5] border-t border-gray-200"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-[#1A1F22]">Key is inside the house</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Door locked behind you</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Try the 5-Minute Lockout Check</td></tr>
              <tr class="bg-white border-t border-gray-200"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-[#1A1F22]">Key is lost</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Someone else may have it</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Get in, then rekey</td></tr>
              <tr class="bg-[#F8F8F5] border-t border-gray-200"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-[#1A1F22]">Key won't turn</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Worn key, dirty lock, or a swollen door</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Stop forcing it and call</td></tr>
              <tr class="bg-white border-t border-gray-200"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-[#1A1F22]">Key snapped in the lock</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Piece stuck in the cylinder</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Don't dig at it. Call for extraction</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <h2 id="the-5-minute-lockout-check" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">The 5-Minute Lockout Check</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          This is what we'd tell a neighbor over the phone. Five quick steps, no tools, and plenty of people get back in before anyone has to drive out.
        </p>
        <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 text-gray-700 mb-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>Walk the whole house.</strong> Back door, side door, garage entry. We lock the front by habit and forget the rest.</li>
          <li><strong>Ask who else lives there.</strong> A roommate, a partner, a teenager upstairs with headphones on. Any of them could open the door.</li>
          <li><strong>Think about the spare.</strong> If it's under the mat, a burglar checks there too. Move it afterward.</li>
          <li><strong>Try the garage keypad,</strong> if you have one. It often opens the inner door.</li>
          <li><strong>Look at the ground-floor windows.</strong> Look, don't climb. Falling off a porch rail is a worse day than a lockout.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Nothing worked? Then the easy part is over.
        </p>
        <h2 id="what-not-to-do-when-you-re-locked-out" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">What Not to Do When You're Locked Out</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Most of the damage we see happens before the locksmith arrives. People get frustrated and start improvising.
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 mb-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>Pry the door or frame:</strong> you'll bend the jamb or split the wood, and a bent strike plate can leave a door sticking for months.</li>
          <li><strong>Break a window:</strong> broken glass cuts hands, and the house stays open until the window is fixed.</li>
          <li><strong>Slide a credit card in a deadbolt:</strong> the trick works on a spring latch and almost never on a deadbolt. It does wreck the card.</li>
          <li><strong>Kick the door:</strong> one kick can crack the frame and the door both.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          A technician who opens the door without touching the hardware gives you the best outcome. For most standard residential locks, non-destructive entry is the normal way in, which is why waiting often saves the door.
        </p>
        <h2 id="when-it-s-time-to-call-a-locksmith" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">When It's Time to Call a Locksmith</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Some lockouts can't wait. A pet is shut inside, the stove might be on, a child is stuck behind the door, or it's cold and dark out. Say so right away when you call.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          A decent call goes like this. You give the address, tell them what lock you have if you know, and describe what happened. They ask whether anyone is inside and tell you roughly when they'll arrive. Once they get there, they'll want to confirm you actually live at the address. Don't take that personally. It's there to protect you.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Emergency service at 1st Capital Locksmith runs by appointment, so call <a href="tel:3027350050" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">(302) 735-0050</a> and we'll set up the visit as fast as we can. Regular hours are Sunday through Thursday 7AM to 10PM and Friday 7AM to 3:45PM. We're closed Saturday.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Our <a href="/services/house-lockout/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">house lockout service</a> covers most standard residential locks, and the technician comes to you. There's more detail on our page for <a href="/services/emergency-house-lockout/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">emergency house lockouts</a>.
        </p>
        <h2 id="how-to-spot-a-locksmith-scam-in-a-hurry" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">How to Spot a Locksmith Scam in a Hurry</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Stressed people click the first ad they see, and lockout scams count on that. Picking a locksmith before you need one helps. It also helps to run a few quick checks on whoever picks up.
        </p>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6 not-prose">
          <table class="w-full text-left text-sm border border-gray-200">
            <thead class="bg-[#1A1F22] text-white">
              <tr><th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Check</th><th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Why it matters</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="text-gray-700">
              <tr class="bg-[#F8F8F5] border-t border-gray-200"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-[#1A1F22]">Does the name on the phone match the website?</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Scam outfits often answer with a generic phrase</td></tr>
              <tr class="bg-white border-t border-gray-200"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-[#1A1F22]">Is there a real local address?</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Ours is 2089 S Dupont Hwy #2, Dover, DE 19901 (visits by appointment only)</td></tr>
              <tr class="bg-[#F8F8F5] border-t border-gray-200"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-[#1A1F22]">Will they explain the job first?</td><td class="px-4 py-3">A reputable company talks through the work before starting</td></tr>
              <tr class="bg-white border-t border-gray-200"><td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-[#1A1F22]">Is the vehicle marked?</td><td class="px-4 py-3">An unmarked car is a warning sign</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          We wrote a longer piece on this, <a href="/blog/how-to-avoid-locksmith-scams/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">how to avoid locksmith scams in Delaware</a>, in case you want to bookmark it. If you think you've been scammed, the Delaware Department of Justice <a href="https://news.delaware.gov/2026/03/02/doj-recognizes-national-consumer-protection-week-2/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">encourages residents to report suspected fraud</a> at de.gov/consumercomplaint.
        </p>
        <h2 id="after-you-get-back-in" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">After You Get Back In</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Getting in is only half of it. What happens next depends on how you ended up outside.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          If the key was locked inside, you're fine. Maybe leave a spare with a neighbor you trust.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          If the key was lost, think about who might find it. Anybody holding that key who knows your address can walk in. Rekeying changes the inside of the lock so the old key stops working, and the hardware stays put. Details are on our <a href="/services/lock-rekeying/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">lock rekeying</a> page.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          And if the lock felt gritty or stiff for weeks beforehand, it's probably wearing out. A technician is already at your door, so that's a good time to deal with it.
        </p>
        <h2 id="quick-ways-to-avoid-the-next-lockout" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Quick Ways to Avoid the Next Lockout</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Nothing fancy here.
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 mb-6 space-y-2">
          <li>Give a spare to a person you trust instead of a hiding spot.</li>
          <li>Consider a keypad deadbolt so the door doesn't hinge on one key. Our post on <a href="/blog/smart-lock-installation-dover-de/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">smart lock installation in Dover</a> walks through the options.</li>
          <li>Pat your pockets before the door closes. Phone, wallet, keys.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          One more thing. Keep every exit usable from the inside. The National Fire Protection Association's <a href="https://www.nfpa.org/-/media/project/storefront/catalog/files/home-fire-safety/emac/educationalmessagesdeskreference.pdf" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2026 educational messages guide</a> says to know two ways out of every room and to make sure every door and window that leads outside opens. A good lock should never keep anyone from getting out.
        </p>
        <h2 id="locked-out-in-dover-and-nearby-towns" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Locked Out in Dover and Nearby Towns</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          We serve <a href="/service-areas/dover-de/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">Dover</a> and the Kent County towns around it: Camden, Smyrna, Felton, Harrington, Magnolia, Frederica, Cheswold, Clayton, Milford and Leipsic. 1st Capital Locksmith is locally owned and operated, bonded and insured, and our technicians come to your door.
        </p>
        <h2 id="faq" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-12 mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div class="space-y-4 not-prose">

          <details class="bg-[#F8F8F5] rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-200 group">
            <summary class="font-bold text-[#1A1F22] text-lg cursor-pointer flex justify-between items-center">
              How do I get into a locked house?
              <span class="text-[#A61E22] text-2xl group-open:rotate-45 transition-transform">+</span>
            </summary>
            <p class="mt-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Check every other door and any ground-floor window you can see, ask anyone inside or anyone with a spare, and try a garage keypad. If none of that works, a locksmith can usually open a standard residential lock without damaging it.</p>
          </details>

